## 7. Create the Standalone EXE

### 7.1 Basic build

Run the following command from the project root:

```powershell
pyinstaller --onefile --windowed --name SAPTicketAssistant sap_ticket_assistant.py
```

Options used:

- `--onefile` creates one executable file.
- `--windowed` prevents a console window from appearing for the Tkinter GUI.
- `--name SAPTicketAssistant` sets the output filename.

The generated EXE will be located at:

```text
dist\SAPTicketAssistant.exe
```

### 7.2 Build with a Windows icon

```powershell
pyinstaller --onefile --windowed --name SAPTicketAssistant --icon assets\sap_ticket_assistant.ico sap_ticket_assistant.py
```

### 7.3 Clean previous outputs before rebuilding

```powershell
pyinstaller --clean --noconfirm --onefile --windowed --name SAPTicketAssistant --icon assets\sap_ticket_assistant.ico sap_ticket_assistant.py
```

PyInstaller may generate:

```text
build\
dist\
SAPTicketAssistant.spec
```

The `.spec` file describes the build configuration and can be stored in source control when a repeatable customized build is required.

---

## 8. Handling Bundled Resource Files

The current application uses standard-library modules and may not require extra runtime files. If future versions use templates, icons, configuration samples, or other read-only assets, include them explicitly.

Example:

```powershell
pyinstaller --onefile --windowed --name SAPTicketAssistant --add-data "assets;assets" sap_ticket_assistant.py
```

On Windows, the source and destination in `--add-data` are separated by a semicolon.

Do not bundle an operational SQLite database as the live writable database. A bundled file in one-file mode is extracted to a temporary runtime location. If a starter database is needed, bundle it only as a template and copy it to the application's writable data directory during first launch.

---

## 9. Optional PyInstaller Spec File

For controlled releases, use the generated `.spec` file to maintain custom settings.

Generate the initial specification:

```powershell
pyi-makespec --onefile --windowed --name SAPTicketAssistant --icon assets\sap_ticket_assistant.ico sap_ticket_assistant.py
```

Then build from it:

```powershell
pyinstaller --clean --noconfirm SAPTicketAssistant.spec
```

A spec file is useful when the build includes:

- Additional resource folders
- Custom icons
- Hidden imports
- Version metadata
- Manifest settings
- Repeated enterprise builds

Avoid passing conflicting build options on the command line when those options are already maintained in the spec file.

