## 25. Build Automation Example

A simple PowerShell build script can standardize the EXE build.

```powershell
$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"

$AppName = "SAPTicketAssistant"
$Source = "sap_ticket_assistant.py"
$Icon = "assets\sap_ticket_assistant.ico"

if (Test-Path "build") {
    Remove-Item "build" -Recurse -Force
}

if (Test-Path "dist") {
    Remove-Item "dist" -Recurse -Force
}

python -m PyInstaller `
    --clean `
    --noconfirm `
    --onefile `
    --windowed `
    --name $AppName `
    --icon $Icon `
    $Source

if (-not (Test-Path "dist\$AppName.exe")) {
    throw "EXE build failed: output file was not found."
}

Write-Host "Build completed: dist\$AppName.exe"
```

Run it from the project directory:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\build.ps1
```

Use only execution-policy settings approved by the organization. In managed environments, the build script should be signed or executed through the approved CI/CD system.

